WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.000
-Greta, welcome back
to the show.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.400
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.701
-I'm so happy to have you.

00:00:06.701 --> 00:00:08.801
You brought a little news,

00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:10.834
exciting news for us.
-Yes.

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:12.934
I am with child.

00:00:12.934 --> 00:00:14.300
-Yes, exactly!

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.501
[ Cheering and applause ]
The best!

00:00:15.501 --> 00:00:16.868
[ Rimshot ]

00:00:16.868 --> 00:00:17.968
Baby Number 2.

00:00:17.968 --> 00:00:19.734
-Number 2.
-You are expecting

00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:21.934
and thank you
for announcing it on our show.

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:24.767
-Yes. Well, I, actually,
I thought it would be --

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:27.400
I went to an event recently
and I wore something

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.334
that I thought everyone would be
so interested to know

00:00:30.334 --> 00:00:33.734
that I was going to have
another child and nobody cared.

00:00:33.734 --> 00:00:35.601
[ Laughter ]
And it didn't get reported on.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:37.501
Turns out nobody's
paying attention to you.

00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:38.534
[ Laughter ]
-No.

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:39.734
No, we are paying attention.

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:42.434
We're so happy for you.
This is going to be

00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:45.901
Baby Harold's brother
or sister, yeah.
-Yes, yeah, yeah.

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:47.067
-He's four years old.

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:49.367
-Almost four, yes.
He's almost four, yeah.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.167
-Oh, no, that's --
I can't believe it's so --

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:52.734
it happened so fast.
-I know.

00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:54.200
-We've known each other
for a long time

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.067
and, now, you have babies.
-I know.

00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:58.334
[ Laughter ]
-What does Baby Harold think
of this new baby?

00:00:58.334 --> 00:01:02.100
-It's hard to know what
a three-year-old makes of things

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.367
that aren't existent, yet,
in front of him.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:05.434
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:06.467
-Yeah.
He's --

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:08.734
I mean, he's beautifully

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:10.400
sophisticated, in some ways,

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.200
and then, in other ways,
he doesn't --

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.701
He asks, "What language do they
speak in New Jersey?"

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.434
[ Laughter ]
So.
-Yeah.

00:01:17.434 --> 00:01:18.834
That's a good question,
actually.

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:20.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:22.334
What did you tell him? Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:23.400
I mean, we were looking

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.300
across the Hudson River
and he said,

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.801
"And what language do they speak
in New Jersey?"

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:29.534
[ Laughter ]
And I thought, "Oh, that's --

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:32.868
I wish I had a wonderful answer
for you in your queries."

00:01:32.868 --> 00:01:34.667
So, the idea of a child
is sort of.

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.467
-But he said something
very deep to you

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:37.901
-Yes.
-that made me --
I wanted you to say

00:01:37.901 --> 00:01:39.300
because I really like this.
It made me laugh.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.701
He was having a bath.

00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:42.801
-He has -- He loves bubbles.

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:45.801
Like all three-year-olds
love bubbles.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:48.567
But he gets very sad
when the bubbles go away

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:50.634
[ Laughter ]
and he starts welling up.

00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:52.534
And then he looked at me
and he said,

00:01:52.534 --> 00:01:54.167
"That's the nature of bubbles."

00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:55.567
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:58.567
And I said, "Oh, that is --"

00:01:58.567 --> 00:01:59.634
-That's the nature.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:01.367
-That's the nature.
-That's the nature.

00:02:01.367 --> 00:02:02.801
-That's the nature of bubbles.

00:02:02.801 --> 00:02:04.267
And I was like, "Well, maybe --"
-That's deep.

00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:05.901
-Yes. Maybe we'll understand.
-That's deep.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:06.767
-But yes.
-Oh, that's so cute.

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:07.801
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:09.100
-I'm so happy for you.
-Thank you.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.200
-I wanted to --
We've been talking about you,

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.400
weirdly, on the show
without you --

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.567
without your permission
because we're such fans of you.

00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:18.100
I know you're --
you're directing "Barbie."

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.367
-Yes.
-Written and directed this.

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:21.701
-That's right.
Yes, I wrote it

00:02:21.701 --> 00:02:22.934
with my partner, Noah...

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:24.667
-Yeah, we love Noah.
-and I directed it, yeah.

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:27.801
-And you directed this.
So, we had everyone on.

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:30.934
We've had Ryan Gosling on.
We had Margot Robbie on.

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:32.901
Talk about --
because this photo went out

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:35.734
-Oh, yes.
-and kind of just
the Internet went nuts.

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:37.901
[ Laughter ]
-Yes, yes, yes. Yes.

00:02:37.901 --> 00:02:40.734
-What did you think about this
when this --

00:02:40.734 --> 00:02:42.634
-Well, first of all, I

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:44.767
just adore everybody who's
in this film

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:46.367
-Yeah.
-and I'm in that

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:49.801
very strange experience
of being a director editing,

00:02:49.801 --> 00:02:52.601
which is I feel like I hang out
with them every day

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:54.133
and they don't feel that way

00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:55.634
[ Laughter ]
because I'm alone

00:02:55.634 --> 00:02:56.934
with footage of their face.

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:58.467
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. So,

00:02:58.467 --> 00:02:59.834
you think you're besties.

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:03.667
-Yeah, I'm like I feel like we
spend all day together.

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:06.601
But, no, that picture was

00:03:06.601 --> 00:03:08.801
we'd shot most of the movie
in London

00:03:08.801 --> 00:03:10.868
and then we went to L.A.
to shoot

00:03:10.868 --> 00:03:15.534
and everything for us was so --

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:16.801
The cast was so committed.

00:03:16.801 --> 00:03:18.634
Everybody gave everything
they had.

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:19.801
They really went for it.

00:03:19.801 --> 00:03:22.334
It's like full, full commitment.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:25.501
And then we realized,
in that moment, we were like,

00:03:25.501 --> 00:03:27.367
"Oh, we look insane!"

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:28.801
[ Laughter ]
But we didn't know.

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:30.234
-No, if you're in the bubble
and everyone's dressed

00:03:30.234 --> 00:03:31.834
like this.
-Yes. Then we were like,

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:33.400
[ Laughter ]
"This is normal."
-You go, "This is normal.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.634
Yeah, this is what
everyone looks like."

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:36.300
And then you get
out and people are like,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.801
"Wh-Wh-What?!"
-Exactly.

00:03:37.801 --> 00:03:40.000
[ Laughter ]
So, it felt like --
And it was suddenly --

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.834
I --
But they were incredible.

00:03:41.834 --> 00:03:43.467
They honestly --
-Did you know you were going
to direct this

00:03:43.467 --> 00:03:46.367
or you were just hired
to write, at first, right?

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:50.267
-We were hired to write it
and I loved writing it

00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:51.934
and Noah and I had the best time
writing it

00:03:51.934 --> 00:03:55.100
and then I just love
Margot Robbie so much

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.767
and I got to know her
as a producer

00:03:56.767 --> 00:03:59.667
because she also produced it
and she's a brilliant actress.

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:01.834
And then it just killed me
to think that anybody else

00:04:01.834 --> 00:04:04.100
would get to hang out with her,
so I was like,

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.267
[ Laughter ]
"I have to. I have to.
-"Can I direct this?" Yeah.

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:08.601
-She's. my fave.
-And everyone cool is

00:04:08.601 --> 00:04:09.734
in this, I just know.
-Yes.

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.434
-Also, Will Ferrell,
we had on the show.

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.133
-Issa Rae.
-I know Will isn't it.

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:14.067
[ Cheering ]
-Yeah. Oh, Issa Rae?

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:15.501
-Issa Rae, Simu Liu...

00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:16.567
-Oh, yeah.
-...Ncuti Gatwa.

00:04:16.567 --> 00:04:18.200
-Oh, my gosh.
-Whoo!

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.200
-Michael Cera.
-Oh, my gosh!

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.534
[ Cheering and whistling ]
-But this is later.

00:04:21.534 --> 00:04:22.801
We'll do this later.
-Yeah, we'll do this all later.

00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:24.434
-Later.
-Now, we have -- Yeah.

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:27.067
We have baby news.
You can't top baby news.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.934
Except, we have to talk
about your movie

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:31.501
-Yes, yes, yes.
-as well, "White Noise."

00:04:31.501 --> 00:04:33.234
-Congrats on the great
reviews already.
-Thank you.

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.901
-This is awesome.
Super fun movie.

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:37.901
-Yes.
-How did you, again,

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:39.267
get involved with this one?

00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:42.367
-Well, my partner,
Noah Baumbach,

00:04:42.367 --> 00:04:46.033
wrote and directed it
and it's based on a book,

00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:48.901
"White Noise," by Don DeLillo,
which was written in 1985.

00:04:48.901 --> 00:04:50.234
-Did you know the book?
-I did!

00:04:50.234 --> 00:04:52.267
I read it when I was 19
and I loved it.

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:54.267
I thought it was outrageous
and hilarious

00:04:54.267 --> 00:04:56.834
and it felt like
about everything.

00:04:56.834 --> 00:04:58.400
-Yeah.
-And it's one of those books

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.267
that, at every moment,
it feels about right now.

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:04.701
And so --
-So, Noah's going,
"I'm going to direct this movie

00:05:04.701 --> 00:05:05.834
and this is going to be great,"

00:05:05.834 --> 00:05:07.200
and you go, "Phew,
it is going to be great."

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.567
And it's like,
"And who are you thinking about

00:05:08.567 --> 00:05:10.501
for the lead, you know?"
And he's like,

00:05:10.501 --> 00:05:11.767
"Well, Adam Driver."

00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:14.133
"Yeah, and how about
the female lead?"

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:15.000
[ Laughter ]
Did you kind of go like,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.234
slip your résumé?
-Yes, yes.

00:05:16.234 --> 00:05:19.300
[ Laughter ]
-"I'd be perfect for this."

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.868
-He was actually
across the apartment.

00:05:21.868 --> 00:05:23.767
He sort of called
to me and said,

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:25.300
"Who should play Babette?!"

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.901
And I said, "Obviously, me!"

00:05:26.901 --> 00:05:28.300
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah!

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:29.434
Exactly the way to do it!

00:05:29.434 --> 00:05:31.033
"I should play Babette," yeah.

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:33.868
-But I think I -- You know,
I hadn't acted in a while

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:36.200
and I got -- you know,
it's nerve-wracking

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.067
and it's with Adam Driver
and Don Cheadle

00:05:40.067 --> 00:05:41.734
-Yes!
-and really amazing actors.

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:44.334
-But you have that wig that
you're wearing, that is so --

00:05:44.334 --> 00:05:45.601
-[ Laughs ]
Wait 'til you see this.

00:05:45.601 --> 00:05:47.200
-The wig is great.
-This is one of your costars.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:48.767
Look at this.
-Yes.

00:05:48.767 --> 00:05:51.033
[ Laughter ]
I wish that's my hair.

00:05:51.033 --> 00:05:53.100
-No, I mean, but this is
a very important part

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.701
of the character, though,
Babette.

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:56.567
-Don says,
about my character, he says,

00:05:56.567 --> 00:05:58.334
"Your wife has important hair."

00:05:58.334 --> 00:06:00.434
[ Laughter ]
And so I feel like,

00:06:00.434 --> 00:06:02.734
as long as I had
the important hair, I'd be safe.

00:06:02.734 --> 00:06:04.000
-You did.
You had the important hair.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.701
You could pull --
You pulled this off, bud.

00:06:05.701 --> 00:06:07.534
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:06:07.534 --> 00:06:09.334
How do we set up the movie here,
"White Noise"?

00:06:09.334 --> 00:06:12.434
-Well, "White Noise"
is a comedy.

00:06:12.434 --> 00:06:16.434
It takes place in the 1980s
and it's about a...

00:06:16.434 --> 00:06:18.234
typical family,

00:06:18.234 --> 00:06:20.834
sort of slightly off,
but they're trying --

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.934
they're facing a disaster
and ignoring it,

00:06:22.934 --> 00:06:24.968
which we might all know
something about.

00:06:24.968 --> 00:06:26.701
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, exactly.
Yeah, of course.

00:06:26.701 --> 00:06:29.100
I want to show a clip.
Here's Greta Gerwig

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.000
in "White Noise."
Take a look at this.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.267
-That stuff causes cancer
in laboratory animals,

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:34.467
in case you didn't know.

00:06:34.467 --> 00:06:36.501
-You wanted me to chew
sugarless gum, Denise.

00:06:36.501 --> 00:06:37.567
It was your idea.

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:38.934
-There was no warning
on the pack then.

00:06:38.934 --> 00:06:40.100
Then they put a warning,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.133
which I have a hard time
believing you didn't see.

00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:44.868
-Either I chew gum with sugar
and artificial coloring

00:06:44.868 --> 00:06:47.133
or I chew sugarless gum
that's harmful to rats.

00:06:47.133 --> 00:06:48.100
It's up to you.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:48.834
-Don't chew at all.

00:06:48.834 --> 00:06:49.834
You ever think of that?

00:06:49.834 --> 00:06:51.367
-Denise!

00:06:51.367 --> 00:06:52.801
Steffie!

00:06:52.801 --> 00:06:54.767
Either I chew gum or I smoke.

00:06:54.767 --> 00:06:55.868
-Why not do both?

00:06:55.868 --> 00:06:57.434
That's what you want, isn't it?

00:06:57.434 --> 00:06:59.234
We all get to do what we want,
don't we?

00:06:59.234 --> 00:07:02.133
-Unless we're not allowed to
because of our age and height.

00:07:02.133 --> 00:07:03.701
-You're making a fuss
over nothing.

00:07:03.701 --> 00:07:04.834
-I guess you're right.

00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:05.667
Never mind.

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:06.934
Just a warning on the pack.

00:07:06.934 --> 00:07:08.133
-Just rats.

00:07:08.133 --> 00:07:09.634
-Just useless rodents.

00:07:09.634 --> 00:07:12.033
Plus, I'd like to believe she
only chews two pieces a day,

00:07:12.033 --> 00:07:13.667
the way she forgets things.

00:07:13.667 --> 00:07:15.167
-What do I forget?

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:17.033
-It's alright.
Never mind.

00:07:17.033 --> 00:07:18.367
[ Cheering and applause ]
[ Laughs, claps ]

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:21.534
Come on.
Greta Gerwig, everybody!

00:07:21.534 --> 00:07:23.734
"White Noise" is
in select theaters now

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:26.033
and on Netflix December 30th.

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:27.801
Dane Cook joins us
after the break.

00:07:27.801 --> 00:07:29.133
Stick around, everybody.

